.TH MEMMON 1 "2026-09-22" "memmon 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
memmon \- scrollable, man-page-styled Linux memory usage monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B memmon
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B memmon \-\-once
[\fB\-\-format\fR \fItext\fR|\fIjson\fR|\fIcsv\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B memmon \-\-daemon
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B memmon \-\-report
\fIDAY\fR|\fIFILE\fR [\fB\-\-format\fR \fItext\fR|\fIjson\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B memmon
reads
.I /proc/meminfo
(total/used/free/available memory, buffers, cache, shared memory, swap,
zswap, active/inactive, reclaimable slab, dirty/writeback, commit, vmalloc,
huge pages, and every other field the running kernel exposes), the load
average, memory pressure stall information
.RI ( /proc/pressure/memory ),
OOM kills, swap-in/out and major fault rates
.RI ( /proc/vmstat ),
zram devices, the cgroup v2 memory limit that applies to it, and the
largest processes by resident memory.
.PP
By default it shows all of this as a scrollable, searchable interactive
display with trend sparklines that refreshes on a timer. It can also print
a single snapshot as text, JSON or CSV
.RB ( \-\-once ),
run as a headless logger under systemd
.RB ( \-\-daemon ),
or summarise a day's log
.RB ( \-\-report ).
.PP
"Used" memory is \fBMemTotal \- MemAvailable\fR, matching \fBfree\fR(1)
and \fBhtop\fR(1).
.SH MODES
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-daemon
Headless mode: no display, just logs a snapshot every interval and raises
alerts (see \fBALERTS\fR). Intended for systemd. SIGTERM/SIGINT stop it
cleanly; SIGHUP (\fBsystemctl reload memmon\fR) re-reads the
configuration.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-once
Print a single snapshot to stdout and exit. No interactive display, no
loop, no log file written. See \fB\-\-format\fR.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-plain
Interactive watch mode without ncurses: plain ANSI clear-screen/redraw
instead of full cursor addressing. Use this if the default ncurses
display looks garbled or fails to redraw in place on your terminal
(seen on some Windows/WSL terminal front-ends).
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-report " " \fIDAY\fR|\fIFILE\fR
Summarise a day's log: number of samples, memory used min/avg/max with the
time of the peak, swap usage, memory pressure, and OOM kills during the
day. \fIDAY\fR is \fBYYYY\-MM\-DD\fR, \fBtoday\fR or \fByesterday\fR and is
looked up in the log directory (then \fI~/.local/share/memmon\fR); text,
JSON Lines and CSV logs are all understood, gzip-compressed or not.
Alternatively give the path of a log file.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interval " " \fIINTERVAL\fR
Refresh/log interval. Accepts plain seconds or a suffix: \fBs\fR (seconds),
\fBm\fR (minutes), \fBh\fR (hours), \fBd\fR (days). Examples: \fB5\fR,
\fB5s\fR, \fB5m\fR, \fB1h\fR. Default: \fB5s\fR. (\fBMEMMON_INTERVAL\fR)
.TP
.B \-\-no\-align
In daemon and plain modes samples are scheduled on a fixed timetable, so
they never drift, and, when the interval divides a day, aligned to
wall-clock multiples of it (\fB:00\fR, \fB:05\fR, \fB:10\fR ... for
\fB5m\fR). This option keeps the fixed timetable but starts it from launch
time instead. (\fBMEMMON_ALIGN\fR=\fBno\fR)
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " \fItext\fR|\fIjson\fR|\fIcsv\fR
Output format for \fB\-\-once\fR (and \fBtext\fR or \fBjson\fR for
\fB\-\-report\fR). JSON is a single object per snapshot; CSV is a header
line plus one row. Default: \fBtext\fR.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-log\-dir " " \fIDIR\fR
Directory for daily log files. Default: \fI/var/log/memmon\fR. When the
directory comes from the default or the configuration and isn't writable
(for example a normal user running the interactive display), interactive
modes fall back to \fI~/.local/share/memmon\fR. A directory given with
\fB\-l\fR (or configured for the daemon) is always used as-is.
(\fBMEMMON_LOG_DIR\fR)
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-log\-format " " \fItext\fR|\fIjson\fR|\fIcsv\fR
Log file format: \fBtext\fR writes \fIYYYY\-MM\-DD.log\fR (human
readable), \fBjson\fR writes \fIYYYY\-MM\-DD.jsonl\fR (one JSON object per
line, including every \fI/proc/meminfo\fR field and the top processes),
\fBcsv\fR writes \fIYYYY\-MM\-DD.csv\fR (fixed columns, header on the first
line). Default: \fBtext\fR. (\fBMEMMON_LOG_FORMAT\fR)
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-log\-retention\-days " " \fIN\fR
Delete log files older than \fIN\fR days automatically. \fBN=0\fR disables
auto-deletion and keeps logs forever. Default: \fB30\fR.
(\fBMEMMON_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS\fR)
.TP
.B \-\-no\-compress
Don't gzip previous days' log files. By default, whenever the log rolls
over to a new day, older \fIYYYY\-MM\-DD.*\fR logs are compressed to
\fI.gz\fR. (\fBMEMMON_LOG_COMPRESS\fR=\fBno\fR)
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-log
Disable logging while in the interactive modes.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-top " " \fIN\fR
Show (and log) the \fIN\fR largest processes by resident set size, with
their swap usage. \fB0\fR hides the section; the maximum is \fB50\fR.
Default: \fB5\fR. (\fBMEMMON_TOP_PROCESSES\fR)
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-warn " " \fIPCT\fR
.TQ
.BR \-C ", " \-\-crit " " \fIPCT\fR
Memory-used thresholds for the warning and critical levels. They set the
colors of the bars and drive \fBALERTS\fR. Defaults: \fB60\fR and
\fB85\fR. (\fBMEMMON_WARN_PERCENT\fR, \fBMEMMON_CRIT_PERCENT\fR)
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-alert\-cmd " " \fICOMMAND\fR
Shell command run by the daemon when the alert level changes; see
\fBALERTS\fR. (\fBMEMMON_ALERT_CMD\fR)
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-cgroup " " \fIPATH\fR
cgroup v2 group to report on, relative to \fI/sys/fs/cgroup\fR. By default
memmon walks up from its own cgroup to the nearest ancestor with a memory
limit (for example the container it runs in) and omits the section when
nothing is limited. (\fBMEMMON_CGROUP\fR)
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-units " " \fIauto\fR|\fIk\fR|\fIm\fR|\fIg\fR
Show sizes in automatically scaled units (default), or always in KiB, MiB
or GiB. (\fBMEMMON_UNITS\fR)
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Read settings from \fIFILE\fR instead of the system and user configuration
files.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Show version and exit.
.SH INTERACTIVE KEYS
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.TP
.B "Up/Down, j/k, mouse wheel"
Scroll.
.TP
.B "PgUp/PgDn, Space"
Scroll one page.
.TP
.B "g / G, Home / End"
Jump to top / bottom of the report.
.TP
.B r
Refresh immediately and reset the countdown.
.TP
.B p
Pause / resume sampling (the display stays scrollable).
.TP
.B u
Cycle size units: auto, KiB, MiB, GiB.
.TP
.B /
Search the report (case-insensitive); the matching line is highlighted.
.TP
.B "n / N"
Next / previous match.
.TP
.B "? or h"
Show the key help.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings are taken from, in increasing order of precedence: built-in
defaults, \fI/etc/memmon/memmon.conf\fR, \fI~/.config/memmon/memmon.conf\fR
(or \fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/memmon/memmon.conf\fR), \fBMEMMON_*\fR environment
variables, and command-line options. \fB\-c\fR replaces both files.
.PP
The files contain \fBKEY=VALUE\fR lines using the variable names shown
with each option above; blank lines and lines starting with \fB#\fR are
ignored, an \fBexport\fR prefix and matching surrounding quotes are
allowed. An invalid value is an error (naming the file and line); an
unknown key is a warning.
.SH ALERTS
The memory-used percentage is classified as \fBok\fR, \fBwarning\fR
(\fB\-\-warn\fR) or \fBcritical\fR (\fB\-\-crit\fR). A level is only left
again once usage drops 5 points below its threshold, so values hovering
around a threshold don't flap. Interactive modes show the level in the
header. In daemon mode each change is written to stderr (the journal) and,
if set, \fB\-\-alert\-cmd\fR is run with \fB/bin/sh \-c\fR, in the
background, with these environment variables:
.TP
.B MEMMON_ALERT_LEVEL, MEMMON_ALERT_PREVIOUS
\fBok\fR, \fBwarning\fR or \fBcritical\fR.
.TP
.B MEMMON_MEM_PCT, MEMMON_MEM_USED_KB, MEMMON_MEM_TOTAL_KB, MEMMON_SWAP_PCT
Current usage.
.TP
.B MEMMON_PSI_SOME_AVG10
Memory pressure, when the kernel provides it.
.TP
.B MEMMON_WARN_PERCENT, MEMMON_CRIT_PERCENT, MEMMON_HOST
Thresholds and host name.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.nf
.RS
MEMMON_ALERT_CMD=logger \-p user.warning "memory $MEMMON_ALERT_LEVEL ($MEMMON_MEM_PCT%)"
.RE
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/log/memmon/YYYY\-MM\-DD.{log,jsonl,csv}[.gz]
Default daily log location (see \fB\-\-log\-dir\fR). Previous days are
compressed and files older than the retention window are deleted
automatically; other files in the directory are never touched.
.TP
.I /etc/memmon/memmon.conf
System-wide configuration, also used by the systemd service. Apply
changes with \fBsystemctl reload memmon\fR.
.TP
.I ~/.config/memmon/memmon.conf
Per-user configuration.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/memmon.service
systemd unit for running memmon as a headless logging daemon as the
unprivileged \fBmemmon\fR user. Enabled and started automatically when
installed via the memmon .deb package. A log directory other than
\fI/var/log/memmon\fR must be writable by that user and allowed with
\fBsystemctl edit memmon\fR (\fB[Service]\fR \fBReadWritePaths=\fR\fIDIR\fR).
.SH EXIT STATUS
\fB0\fR on success, \fB1\fR on a runtime error (for example no readable
\fI/proc/meminfo\fR, or no logs for \fB\-\-report\fR), \fB2\fR for invalid
options or configuration.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
memmon
Interactive display, refreshing every 5 seconds.
.TP
memmon \-i 5m
Interactive display, refreshing every 5 minutes.
.TP
memmon \-\-once \-\-format json | jq .mem_pct
One-shot snapshot as JSON.
.TP
memmon \-\-daemon \-i 5m \-F csv
Headless logger writing CSV.
.TP
memmon \-\-report yesterday
Summary of yesterday's log.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR free (1),
.BR top (1),
.BR proc (5),
.BR systemd.exec (5)
